With `piper_plot()` we can create custom piper plots using data
from EMS but with the option to add grouping variables from other data sets.

This vignette will walk you through how to create custom piper plots using the
`piper_plot()` function in R.

> Note: Currently you can only customize your piper plots in R (not via the
Shiny app).

Let's get started!

First we'll load the packages we want to use

```{r setup}
#| message = FALSE
library(bcgwcat2) # To download EMS data and create piper plots
library(dplyr) # To manipulate our data
library(lubridate) # To manipulate dates
```


Now let's get our data by loading a couple of stations' data.
These are from aquifer 259 (1401030 and 1401377) and aquifer 662 (E292373Q)

```{r}
s <- fetch_renmods(ems_ids = c("1401030", "1401377", "E292373"))
```

Let's take a quick look... lots of data!
```{r}
s
```

```{r}
ss <- fmt_minimal(s)
```

## Groups

By default, the piper plot creates a plot with points coloured by EMS ID.

```{r}
piper_plot(samples = ss)
```

We can override this by specifying the `group` argument ourselves. In this
case, let's create a column `Year` and colour points that.

```{r}
ss <- mutate(ss, Year = factor(year(observed_date_time)))

piper_plot(ss, group = "Year")
```

## Appearance

We can also customize the appearance of the points.

First let's create some year categories to group by. We'll also turn this variable
into a factor (category) and specify the order of the levels so our legend is
in the correct order.

```{r}
ss <- mutate(
  ss,
  Year = year(observed_date_time),
  Year_cat = case_when(
    Year < 2010 ~ "Pre 2010s",
    Year >= 2010 & Year <= 2015 ~ "2010-2015",
    Year > 2015 ~ ">2015"
  ),
  Year_cat = factor(Year_cat, levels = c("Pre 2010s", "2010-2015", ">2015"))
)
```

Note that we need to match the number of groups to the number of colours,
shapes, etc. or supply only one value. For example, here we want all the points
to be large and open, so we specify `point_size = 5` and use the shapes 0, 2, and 1.
See the [ggplot2 documentation](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/articles/ggplot2-specs.html?q=shape#sec:shape-spec) for which shapes correspond to which points, noting that 
some are filled and some are empty.

```{r}
piper_plot(
  samples = ss,
  group = "Year_cat",
  point_colour = c("#21908C", "#440154", "#9AD93C"),
  point_shape = c(0, 2, 1),
  point_size = 5
)
```

Hmm, those points are a bit tough to see, let's use filled shapes.

```{r}
piper_plot(
  samples = ss,
  group = "Year_cat",
  point_colour = c("#21908C", "#440154", "#9AD93C"),
  point_shape = c(22, 24, 21),
  point_size = 5
)
```

Or specify the shapes with text, "square", "triangle", "circle"
```{r}
piper_plot(
  samples = ss,
  group = "Year_cat",
  point_colour = c("#21908C", "#440154", "#9AD93C"),
  point_shape = c("square", "triangle", "circle"),
  point_size = 5
)
```

Perhaps with a bit of transparency? We can add this by adding two digits to the
end of our colour codes. `"#21908C"` -> `"#21908C90`" adds a touch of transparency.
(We can also omit `point_filled = TRUE` as points are filled by default.)

```{r}
piper_plot(
  samples = ss,
  group = "Year_cat",
  point_colour = c("#21908C90", "#44015490", "#9AD93C90"),
  point_shape = c(22, 24, 21),
  point_size = 5
)
```

Finally we can adjust the legend position and title
```{r}
piper_plot(
  samples = ss,
  group = "Year_cat",
  point_colour = c("#21908C90", "#44015490", "#9AD93C90"),
  point_shape = c(22, 24, 21),
  point_size = 5,
  legend_position = "top",
  legend_title = "Year"
)
```

We can put the legend anywhere we want. Think about the plot area as running
from -1 to 1 from left to right (x) and -1 to 1 from top to bottom (y).
We then specify the (x,y) coordinates that we want. You can even go out of the
plotting area by specifying numbers out of the -1 to 1 range.

```{r}
piper_plot(
  samples = ss,
  group = "Year_cat",
  point_colour = c("#21908C90", "#44015490", "#9AD93C90"),
  point_shape = c(22, 24, 21),
  point_size = 5,
  legend_position = c(0.1, 0.8),
  legend_title = "Year"
)
```

## Quality Control

There are three arguments for specifying quality control (`qc`) which are all TRUE by default:
- `qc_valid_only` - Only include points with valid charge balances (i.e. absolute value <= 10)
- `qc_omit_outliers` - Omit samples within a group which do not have a Dominant water type (see [`dominant_water_type()`](reference/dominant_water_types.html) for details)
- `qc_with_alkalinity` - If HCO3 (`bicarbonate_alkalinity_as_caco3_meq/l`) is missing, use Alkalinity (`alkalinity_total_4.5_as_caco3_meq/l`) instead (see [`water_type()`](reference/water_type.html) for details).

You can turn these off by using `FALSE` for the argument values

```{r}
piper_plot(
  samples = ss,
  qc_omit_outliers = FALSE,
  qc_with_alkalinity = FALSE
)
```